  "observations": [
    "All three platforms pass the ten authority checks, the separate denied WebView and the remote top-level navigation denial, with empty CSP violation lists.",
    "Linux and macOS: cross-origin and opaque frames have no native bootstrap (native-invoke-unavailable). That proves bootstrap absence, not a raw IPC denial.",
    "Windows: subframes receive the native bootstrap (documented Wry/Tauri behaviour). Cross-origin and opaque frames really attempt IPC and are denied by the ACL or origin check; the same-origin frame invokes from the frame itself. Windows therefore exercises a stronger denial path than Linux/macOS, and also confirms why ticket delivery uses main-document evaluation rather than initialization scripts.",
    "Same-origin frames access the parent realm on every platform; only forged-ticket rejection is proven. A bearer ticket is not frame identity.",
    "A real reload advances the document generation and rejects the previous ticket on all three platforms."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "Raw IPC from a Worker or constructed requests is still untested.",
    "Linux Started corresponds to WebKit Committed in Wry 0.55.1; attempted or failed navigation, redirects, BFCache, crashes and window close are not shown to revoke.",
    "No storage or editor permission is established by ping and ACL results.",
    "Platform results come from the hosted runners listed here only."
